 1: 13.0 km" [:br {}] "Intermediate 2: 30.5 km" [:br {}] "Intermediate 3: 42.0 km" [:br {}] "Maximum Climb: ?" [:br {}] "Total Climbing: ?"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The 50 km event took place two days after the " [:a {:href "/results/1907"} "18 km"] ". The snow conditions were difficult, as temperature changes forced most of the skiers to re-wax their skies up to three times during the race. The Swedes hoped to avenge their disappointing results in the short distance event. Their favorites were " [:a {:href "/athletes/85547"} "Gunnar Eriksson"] ", World Champion in 1950, and the defending Olympic champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/87794"} "Nils Karlsson"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "At 13 km, World Champion Eriksson, who started eighth, was a clear leader, almost a minute ahead of two other early starters, Norwegians " [:a {:href "/athletes/95709"} "Harald Maartmann"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/99307"} "Olav Økern"] ". However, Eriksson got into trouble with his skis and ended in 12th place. Økern, a veteran at 40-years-old, had spent over two years as a prisoner in Germany during the war. By 30.5 km, things had changed. The Finn " [:a {:href "/athletes/86390"} "Veikko Hakulinen"] ", aged 27 but still a newcomer on the international scene, had taken the lead, 20 seconds faster than the Norwegian " [:a {:href "/athletes/85573"} "Magnar Estenstad"] ". At 42 km, Hakulinen had increased his lead to 2:10, with Estenstad still in second position. The 33-year-old Finn " [:a {:href "/athletes/88116"} "Eero Kolehmainen"] " had moved up to third position, 50 seconds behind Estenstad."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The first skier to finish was the first starter, " [:a {:href "/athletes/95546"} "Kalevi Mononen"] " of Finland, but, a few minutes later, Økern was able to beat his time and take over the lead. Then, Hakulinen finished in superb style with the famous final time of 3:33.33, bettering Økern’s time by over five minutes. Estenstad was able to keep ahead of Økern, but then the final starter, Kolehmainen, had a strong finish, beating Estenstad by 17 seconds and securing a Finnish double. The Finnish coach, " [:a {:href "/athletes/98104"} "Veli Saarinen"] ", had a well organised crew around the track, about 300 ”Finnish tourists”, including about seven with a radio. His team would regularly report snow conditions around the track, allowing for the optimal choice for re-waxing the skis (which might one minute to do, but would gain several minutes in ski time). Also, Saarinen was able to report tactics and split times to the skiers."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button779106", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button779106').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Intermediate 1"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Intermediate 2"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Intermediate 3"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "17"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/86390"} "Veikko Hakulinen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "3-33:33"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "50:50 (8)"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "2-03:20 (1)"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "2-59:50 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "37"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/88116"} "Eero Kolehmainen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "3-38:11"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "51:30